    <string name="gf_deploy">DEPLOY</string>
    <string name="gf_deploy_body">Files land in the game\'s UE4 config folder. Every overwrite is backed up first, every push is verified by md5 read-back, and the game\'s own config hash monitor is re-synced (root/Shizuku only). The game is stopped first — it rewrites its configs from memory on exit.</string>
    <string name="gf_deploy_shell">DEPLOY (ROOT / SHIZUKU)</string>
    <string name="gf_deploying">DEPLOYING…</string>
    <string name="gf_deploy_saf">DEPLOY VIA SAF</string>
    <string name="gf_deploy_saf_pick">DEPLOY VIA SAF (PICK FOLDER FIRST)</string>
    <string name="gf_deploy_noshell">Root/Shizuku unavailable — SAF only. SAF cannot stop the game or sync the hash monitor, and it needs the game\'s Android data folder picked once.</string>
    <string name="gf_backups">BACKUPS</string>
    <string name="gf_backups_body">The bytes the device actually held before each overwrite, newest first. Restore pushes them back through the shell channel.</string>
    <string name="gf_restore">RESTORE</string>
    <string name="gf_history">DEPLOY HISTORY</string>
    <string name="gf_clear_all">CLEAR ALL</string>
    <string name="gf_history_body">Every deploy and restore, newest first, with what the device actually did: channel, per-file md5 as the device computed it, game stop and hash-monitor outcome. Verify re-reads each file\'s md5 now and compares it with the recorded one — \'changed since deploy\' is a measured fact, not a guess. (For a measured before/after FPS comparison across deploys, use the AUTO-TUNE card above — it is exactly that loop, one Client.log read per round.)</string>
